{"reference":"Joel 1","book":"Joel","chapter":1,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Joel portrays more than damaged crops. Locust consumption, drought and fire leave seed, harvest, food, drink offerings, pasture, and watercourses depleted; people, livestock, and wild animals all suffer within the same cascading agrarian crisis. The chapter’s repeated images of withering and loss therefore make the summons to lament a response to material devastation, not merely symbolic scenery.","verse_refs":["Joel 1:1-20","Joel 1:1-4","Joel 1:13-20"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_joel_001_1e8613a4e9f3e0b3b9035e19"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"A communal response to crisis","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The appeal moves from elders and all inhabitants to priests and the gathered community. Remembering the catastrophe across generations, lamenting in sackcloth, fasting, assembling, and crying out to Jehovah are presented as communal acts. Priests are called to lament and intercede, while the loss of grain and drink offerings shows that the crisis has also disrupted worship at the house of Jehovah.","verse_refs":["Joel 1:1-4","Joel 1:13-14","Joel 1:5-20"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_joel_001_ef56f822729df376030426f8"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Questions the chapter leaves open","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Verse 1 names Joel as the son of Pethuel but gives no office, hometown, king, or biography. Likewise, the four locust terms in verse 4 stress successive, comprehensive consumption, but they do not securely identify four species or four life stages. Proposed dates for Joel range widely, with many scholars favoring a Persian-period or comparably late setting; the chapter itself does not settle the question.","verse_refs":["Joel 1:1-4"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_joel_001_ccbf478f68d6b8062eef17f2"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Reading the disaster carefully","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Joel names no catalogue of individual offenses behind the locust and drought crisis. The chapter should therefore not be used to identify people affected by disaster as uniquely guilty. Its attention to land, crops, water, and animals also keeps the suffering concrete, while not authorizing claims that any modern disaster has a revealed one-to-one divine cause.","verse_refs":["Joel 1:1-20","Joel 1:1-4","Joel 1:5-20"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_joel_001_8ad8823a4421c7954770d13b"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}